ayer across has a T1-T2-T3, but T9 is a terminal. \n\nAlternatively, playing F2 (2 left) is also an option. \n\nBut F2 is South Wind, the prevalent wind. If I discard it, someone might need it for a Pung. \n\nBut I have two F2s. Discarding one might not be bad. \n\nIf I keep one F2, it could contribute to the prevalent wind scoring. \n\nSo, the best options are to play W9, T9, or F2. \n\nConsidering the unshown counts and the likelihood of others needing the tile, playing W9 or T9 is better. \n\nBetween W9 and T9, since both have 3 left, I might choose based on which is less likely to be needed. \n\nLooking at the melds of others: \n\n- The player across has a T1-T2-T3, so they might have used T1, T2, T3. They might not need T9. \n\n- The next player has Pungs of B6 and B8. \n\n- The previous player has a Kong of B5. \n\nSo, T9 is less likely to be needed. \n\nTherefore, playing T9 might be safer. \n\nAlternatively, playing W9. \n\nBut I have two W9s in hand. If I play one, it's a terminal tile. \n\nGiven that, I'll choose to play T9, as it's a terminal and less likely to be needed by others.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The hand has two Chows and needs two more melds and a pair. Playing T9 (terminal tile with 3 remaining) is safest as it's less likely to be needed by others.  \nReason: T9 is a terminal tile with 3 remaining, reducing risk of aiding opponents. Other options like F3 or T1 have lower remaining counts and higher risk.  \nAnswer: Play T9"}
