use Penging F2 gives only 3 points, which is not enough. But I need to check if there are any other ways to reach 8 points. \n\nWait, maybe if I have two chows and a pung, plus a pair, and the pair is a terminal, which gives 1 point for Pung of Terminals or Honors. Wait, the pung is F2 (honor), so that's 1 point. The pair could be a terminal, which is another 1 point. So total from pung and pair would be 2 + 1 (Prevalent Wind) + 1 (Pung of Honors) + 1 (pair as honor or terminal). Wait, no. The Pung of Honors is 1 point, the Prevalent Wind is 2 points. The pair being a terminal would be Pung of Terminals or Honors, but it's a pair, not a pung. Wait, the pattern \"Pung of Terminals or Honors\" applies to pungs or kongs. A pair doesn't count. So the pair being a terminal doesn't add any points. \n\nSo total points from Penging would be 3 (2 from Prevalent Wind, 1 from Pung of Honors). Still 3 points. \n\nTherefore, it's not enough. So passing might be better. \n\nBut why is the option to Peng F2 available? Maybe because the next player discarded F2, and I can form a pung. But if it doesn't help me reach 8 points, then maybe I should pass. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another pattern I'm missing. For example, if I have two chows and a pung, plus a pair of F2, and the pair is part of a concealed hand. But I'm not sure. \n\nAlternatively, if I have a concealed hand (all melds are concealed), but since I have two chows (which are concealed if I formed them myself), and a pung from Peng, which is an exposed meld. So the pung is exposed, so the concealed hand pattern would not apply. \n\nHmm. Given all that, it seems that Penging F2 gives me 3 points, but I need at least 8. So maybe I should pass and try to build other patterns. \n\nBut the valid actions are only Peng F2 or Pass. So I have to choose one. \n\nIn this case, since Penging gives some points but not enough, and passing allows me to keep the hand as is (which has zero points), but maybe I can draw another tile